        "content": "## Problem Statement\n\n**Exercise 1:** Given the function $f(x) = x^2 + 2x + 1$, find:\n\n1. The *derivative* $f'(x)$\n2. The **integral** $\\int f(x)\\,dx$\n\n### Solution\n\nFirst, note that\n$$\nf(x) = x^2 + 2x + 1 = (x+1)^2.\n$$\n\n- The derivative is:\n  $$\n  f'(x) = 2x + 2\n  $$\n\n- The integral is:\n  $$\n  \\int f(x)\\,dx = \\int (x^2 + 2x + 1)\\,dx = \\frac{x^3}{3} + x^2 + x + C\n  $$\n\n---\n\n## Linear Algebra with Lists\n\nFor the matrix\n$$\nA = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix},\n$$\nwe have:\n\n- **Determinant**:\n  $$\n  \\det(A) = ad - bc\n  $$\n\n- **Trace**:\n  $$\n  \\operatorname{tr}(A) = a + d\n  $$\n\n- **Inverse** (if $ad - bc \\ne 0$):\n  $$\n  A^{-1} = \\frac{1}{ad - bc}\n  \\begin{pmatrix}\n  d & -b \\\\\n  -c & a\n  \\end{pmatrix}\n  $$\n\n---\n\n## Real-World Context\n\n> **Note:** In economics, the cost function $C(q) = 100 + 5q$ means fixed costs are **\\$100** and variable costs are **\\$5 per unit**.\n\nIf you buy $10$ units, then:\n$$\nC(10) = 100 + 5(10) = 150\n$$\n\nSo the total cost is **\\$150**.",
